<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hunayn ibn Ishaq</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">808–873</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Johannitius, Joannitius, Hunain ibn Ishaq, Hunayn ibn Ishaq al-Ibadi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hunayn ibn Ishaq al-Ibadi, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as Johannitius, was an Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Nestorian Christian</a> translator and physician of the Abbasid period, his era's most productive translator of Greek medical and scientific works. Born in al-Hirah to the ʿIbad, an Arab community of the Church of the East, he studied medicine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and mastered Greek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, Arabic and Persian. Called the Sheikh of the Translators, he translated 129 works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a> and works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> into Syriac and Arabic, and his Ten Treatises of the Eye is the first known systematic treatment of ophthalmology. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-Mutawakkil made him his physician, but imprisoned him for a year for refusing to make a poison.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Syriac scholars translated Greek philosophy, medicine and science, and in Abbasid Baghdad Syriac Christians such as <b>Hunayn ibn Ishaq</b> translated these works into Arabic, a crucial link in the transmission of Greek learning to the Islamic world and later to Europe.</blockquote><a href="/language/aramaic">Aramaic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Eight generations of the Christian Bukhtishu family served the caliphs as physicians, the Christian <b>Hunayn ibn Ishaq</b> translated Galen into Arabic, and Christian secretaries and Jewish bankers served the government.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li></ul></section>
